fix: decide the last two legacy disagreements; name a thin-dual-domain limit

Both remaining legacy-diff entries disagreed about the DOMAIN only, with zero
value disagreements anywhere. Decided with a sup sampled over the domain at two
reaches -- a LOWER bound, so a finite value below it is impossible, and a
claimed +infinity the samples climb toward is credible.

examples2(9): THE EXACT ROUTE IS RIGHT AND THE LEGACY IS WRONG, at 109 of 203
points. conjQ says +infinity; the legacy returns finite values the sampled sup
already exceeds, and the samples keep climbing as the reach grows (17 -> 2330,
5 -> 1047, 9 -> 1375). Worth recording that the legacy is not a reliable oracle
on this family: it also raises its own Step-3-against-Step-2 cross-check on
examples(11).

examples(19): the LEGACY is right and conjQ is wrong, at ONE point. Nine affine
pieces on a fan whose dual domain is the single point s = (0,0), where
f*(0,0) = -inf f = 0; conjQ answers +infinity.

KNOWN LIMITATION, now named rather than left as a mystery: assembleQuaConCells
drops cells with no two-dimensional interior, so a conjugate whose whole domain
is thin comes back with zero faces. Relaxing the filter to "nonempty as a set"
was TRIED and is measurably WORSE -- it kept 982 degenerate cells and eval's
tolerance then admitted points genuinely outside the domain, turning one wrong
point into two (f*(1,1) became 2 where it is +infinity). Reverted, with the
measurement recorded at the filter so the next reader does not retry it.

The real fix is to detect a thin dual domain and emit it with EQUALITY sides --
machinery that already exists, and that caseAFullDomain uses for the point and
line cases. What is missing is recognising the situation when it arises from the
FOLD rather than from a single full-plane piece.

Verified: fast 473/0 in 153 s; consistency sweep 0 of 29.

